@* The Help & glossary tab: plain-language explanations of every RogueDeck concept, followed by reference tables
   GENERATED from the engine's own catalogs (CombatProgramModel / StudioVocabulary / RunEventCatalog /
   RelicCombatTriggers) — so the reference can never drift from what the editors actually offer. Static content:
   no document access, no interactivity. *@

<section class="glossary" style="padding:1rem; max-width:72rem">
    <TabIntro Title="Help & glossary"
              Text="What everything in RogueDeck Studio means, in plain language. The reference tables below are generated straight from the engine's catalogs, so they always match what the editors offer."
              Connects="Every dropdown in Studio reads “Plain label (technicalKey)” — the technical key in parentheses is exactly what appears in the project's JSON." />

    <p><a href="help?tour=1">↻ Take the first-visit tour again</a></p>

    <h2>Core concepts</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt>Run</dt>
        <dd>One full playthrough: the hero travels a map of nodes, fights, shops, collects cards and relics, and
            wins or loses. Everything is authored into one shared project document and played from the Run tab.</dd>
        <dt>Map &amp; nodes</dt>
        <dd>The run's structure — a graph of nodes connected by paths the player chooses between. A node is a fight
            (combat), a story moment (event) or a shop.</dd>
        <dt>Card &amp; deck</dt>
        <dd>A card is something the player plays in a fight; what it does is its effect program. The deck is the
            set of cards carried through the run, drawn into a hand each fight.</dd>
        <dt>Encounter</dt>
        <dd>One battle: which enemies appear, their health, and which actions they take (fixed rotation, or picked
            by intent rules based on the fight's state).</dd>
        <dt>Enemy action &amp; intent</dt>
        <dd>One thing an enemy can do — an effect program — plus the intent (label + kind) telegraphed to the
            player before it happens.</dd>
        <dt>Relic</dt>
        <dd>A passive item the player keeps. It reacts to run events (e.g. gain gold after every fight) and can add
            standing combat rules to every fight while owned (e.g. thorns).</dd>
        <dt>Status</dt>
        <dd>A buff or debuff sitting on a unit (poison, strength…). It can passively modify values, run a program
            when something happens to its bearer, and intercept effects (block debuffs, prevent death).</dd>
        <dt>Consumable</dt>
        <dd>A one-use item (potion). Its use effects run once when the player uses it.</dd>
        <dt>Event</dt>
        <dd>A story moment with choices; each choice lists what it does.</dd>
        <dt>Shop</dt>
        <dd>A place to spend gold — buy-once items, paid rerolls, and services like card removal.</dd>
        <dt>Shred (card part)</dt>
        <dd>A piece cards are built from: it fills 1–6 of a card's 6 spaces, contributes cost, and runs its
            effect fragment at its position when the built card is played (top part first). A shred can also
            modify its neighbours' costs ("half the cost of the parts below").</dd>
        <dt>Workbench</dt>
        <dd>The crafting station (a map node): the player adds collected shreds in order — the order IS the
            card — then finishes the card. The parts are consumed; the composed card joins the deck.</dd>
        <dt>Recipe</dt>
        <dd>A curated shred combination (order-blind, duplicates count). Building exactly those parts yields
            the recipe's authored card instead of the raw composition — and unlocks the recipe permanently,
            so later runs can build it directly from its parts.</dd>
        <dt>Meta-progression</dt>
        <dd>Permanent progress between runs, written to the saved profile when a run ends — unlock flags and
            meta-currency counters.</dd>
        <dt>Effect program</dt>
        <dd>A small visual script: steps (deal damage, apply status…), each with a target and an amount, plus
            control flow (sequences, loops, ifs). Cards, enemy actions, relic rules, status triggers and
            consumables all run effect programs.</dd>
        <dt>Target (selector)</dt>
        <dd>Who a step affects — the chosen target, every enemy, the weakest ally… See the table below.</dd>
        <dt>Amount</dt>
        <dd>How much a step does — a fixed number, a value read from the fight (current HP, status stacks…), or
            arithmetic combining both.</dd>
        <dt>Trigger</dt>
        <dd>The moment something reacts: relic reactions hook run events, while relic combat rules and status
            triggers hook combat events.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Worked examples — one per tab</h2>
    <p class="muted">Each example builds something deliberately non-trivial, step by step, using the exact labels
        the editors show. Together they touch most of what the engine can do.</p>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Hero</strong> — a rage-fueled bruiser with a deployable turret</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Set <em>Name</em> to <code>Bruiser</code>, <em>Max HP</em> 70, <em>Starting HP</em> 70.</li>
            <li>Under <em>Combat resources</em>, keep <code>standard.energy</code> (start 3, max 3, refill each turn
                ticked) and add a second resource id <code>rage</code>: start 0, max 10, refill each turn
                <em>unticked</em> — rage persists across turns and only grows when cards grant it.</li>
            <li>Tick a starting relic and add a starting consumable so the run begins equipped.</li>
            <li>Under starting board units, add a unit: <em>Definition id</em> <code>turret</code>, <em>Name</em>
                Turret, <em>Max HP</em> 12; tick <em>placed on grid</em> and set X 0, Y 1; add an innate status
                (e.g. <code>thorns</code>, 2 stacks). The turret now fights beside the hero in every battle.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">Cards can then read or spend rage with the amount “Resource (currentResource)”.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Cards</strong> — “Rampage”: damage that scales with your missing health</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add a card: id <code>rampage</code>, name Rampage, energy 2.</li>
            <li>In its program, set the step to <strong>Deal damage (dealDamage)</strong> and the target to
                <strong>Toughest enemy (highestHealthEnemy)</strong>.</li>
            <li>For the amount, pick <strong>× (mul)</strong> — two operand widgets appear.</li>
            <li>Left operand: <strong>Missing HP (missingHealth)</strong> read from
                <strong>Self / the acting unit (source)</strong>. Right operand:
                <strong>Constant (const)</strong> = 2.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">The card now deals (max HP − current HP) × 2 to the biggest enemy — the lower your health,
            the harder it hits. Add copies of it to the starting deck below the card list.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Characters</strong> — a free Knight and a Mage locked behind meta-progression</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add character <code>knight</code>: its own deck of basic strikes/blocks, 80 HP, a defensive relic.</li>
            <li>Add character <code>mage</code>: 55 HP, a spell-heavy deck, and set <em>Unlock flag</em> to
                <code>unlock.character.mage</code>.</li>
            <li>The Meta example below sets exactly that flag on a victorious run — until then the Mage is not
                offered at run start.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">With a non-empty roster the run starts from the CHOSEN character; the Hero tab's setup is
            used only when the roster is empty.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Encounters</strong> — “The Enrager”: an enemy that changes tactics below half health</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>At the top of the tab, author two enemy actions: <code>claw</code> — intent label Claw, kind Attack,
                program <strong>Deal damage (dealDamage)</strong> → <strong>The chosen target (eventTarget)</strong>,
                amount 6; and <code>frenzy</code> — intent label Frenzy, kind Buff, program
                <strong>Apply status (applyStatus)</strong> → <strong>Self / the acting unit (source)</strong>,
                status id <code>strength</code>, 3 stacks.</li>
            <li>Add an encounter <code>enrager-fight</code>, add an enemy <code>enrager</code> with HP 40 and tick
                both actions.</li>
            <li>Under <em>Intent AI</em>, add a rule: if <strong>HP % (healthPercent)</strong> <code>&lt;</code> 50
                → do <code>frenzy</code>, priority 10. With no rule matching, the enemy cycles its actions — so
                above half health it claws, below it starts stacking strength.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">Conditions nest: wrap the rule in <strong>All of… (allOf)</strong> to also require a round
            number or a status before frenzying.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Relics</strong> — “Bloodpact”: heals after won fights, turns pain into block during them</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add relic <code>bloodpact</code>.</li>
            <li>Reaction: <em>When</em> <strong>A combat resolves (combatResolved)</strong>, <em>if</em>
                <strong>combat was a victory</strong> → add a <strong>Heal</strong> effect, amount 5.</li>
            <li>Combat rule: <em>on</em> <strong>Damage is taken (damageReceived)</strong> → program
                <strong>Gain block (gainBlock)</strong> on <strong>Self / the acting unit (source)</strong> with
                amount <strong>Event amount (event)</strong> — every hit grants that much block.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">Reactions hook run events (the “Run events” table below); combat rules hook combat
            triggers (the “Relic combat triggers” table). “Event amount” is the number the trigger carried — here,
            the damage just taken.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Events</strong> — “The Shrine”: a choice with a tempting, cursed follow-up</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add event <code>shrine</code>; its first situation <code>start</code>, text “A weathered shrine hums
                with power.”</li>
            <li>Choice <code>pray</code>, button text “Pray (heal 15)”: add a <strong>Heal</strong> 15 effect,
                <em>then</em> = (end).</li>
            <li>Choice <code>desecrate</code>, button text “Smash it (gain 80 gold…)”: add <strong>Gold</strong> 80,
                and set <em>then</em> to a second situation <code>curse</code>.</li>
            <li>Situation <code>curse</code>, text “The shrine's spirit brands you.”, one choice “Accept your fate”
                with <strong>Add card</strong> → a curse card authored on the Cards tab, then (end).</li>
            <li>Set <em>Start</em> to <code>start</code> and place the event on the Run tab's map.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Statuses</strong> — “Frostbrand”: a debuff that saps damage and punishes the turn start</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add status <code>frostbrand</code>, <em>Polarity</em> Debuff, <em>Stacking</em>
                <strong>Applications merge (stacks add up)</strong>, tick <em>stacks</em> and <em>duration</em>.</li>
            <li>Passive modifier: pipeline <strong>Damage the bearer deals (DamageDealt)</strong>, operation
                <strong>Add amount × stacks (AddPerStack)</strong>, magnitude −1 — each stack lowers the bearer's
                hits by 1.</li>
            <li>Trigger: <em>On</em> <strong>The bearer's turn starts (TurnStarted)</strong> → program
                <strong>Deal damage (dealDamage)</strong> on <strong>Self / the acting unit (source)</strong>,
                amount <strong>Status stacks (statusStacks)</strong> of <code>frostbrand</code> on self — the brand
                burns for its own stack count.</li>
            <li>Apply it from any card or enemy action with <strong>Apply status (applyStatus)</strong>, status id
                <code>frostbrand</code>, 2 stacks, 3 turns.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Consumables</strong> — “Battle Brew”: nothing now, 20 block when the next fight starts</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add consumable <code>battle-brew</code>, name Battle Brew.</li>
            <li>Under <em>On use:</em> click <strong>next-combat opening</strong> — an embedded combat program
                appears labeled “next combat — at the hero's first turn start”.</li>
            <li>Set it to <strong>Gain block (gainBlock)</strong> on
                <strong>Self / the acting unit (source)</strong>, amount 20.</li>
            <li>Hand it out from an event choice, a shop item, or the Hero tab's starting consumables. Drinking it
                does nothing visible — the payoff arrives at the next fight's first turn.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Shops</strong> — “The Black Market”: small window, pricey rerolls, card removal</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add shop <code>black-market</code>; currency <code>standard.gold</code>, <em>Show at once</em> 3.</li>
            <li>Add five items: two attack cards (60 each), a defensive card (45), and two relics (140, 180) — with
                only 3 shown, each visit sees a different slice of the pool.</li>
            <li>Tick <em>reroll</em>, price 25 — redrawing the display costs gold and prices add up fast.</li>
            <li>Tick <em>card removal</em>, price 75 — the player can pay to slim the deck instead of growing it.</li>
            <li>Point a <strong>Shop</strong> node at it on the Run tab's map.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Meta</strong> — unlock the Mage and bank leftover gold between runs</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add a rule, tick <em>Victory</em>: effect <strong>Set flag (setFlag)</strong> with flag
                <code>unlock.character.mage</code> — winning once permanently unlocks the Mage on the Characters
                tab.</li>
            <li>Add a second rule with <em>no</em> outcome ticked (= fires on any run end): effect
                <strong>Promote run resource (promoteResource)</strong>, run resource <code>standard.gold</code> →
                counter <code>meta-currency</code> — every run's leftover gold banks into a permanent counter.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted">The rules write to the saved profile when a run ends; nothing here affects the run
            itself.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Run</strong> — a branching act: risk the elite or take the safe road</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Add five nodes: a <strong>Fight (combat)</strong> pointing at an easy encounter; a
                <strong>Story event (event)</strong> pointing at the Shrine; a second <strong>Fight</strong> at the
                Enrager (the elite); a <strong>Shop</strong> at the Black Market; a final <strong>Fight</strong> as
                the boss.</li>
            <li>Add edges so the first fight forks: fight-1 → shrine <em>and</em> fight-1 → enrager; then shrine →
                shop, enrager → shop, shop → boss. Set the entry to fight-1.</li>
            <li>Press <em>Auto-layout</em> to see the fork in the map graphic, then <strong>Load &amp; drive</strong>
                to play it — after the first fight the run offers the choice of path.</li>
            <li>Use <strong>Simulate</strong> over many seeds to check both paths are survivable.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Playtest</strong> — tune one fight without touching the map</summary>
        <ol>
            <li>Pick the Enrager encounter from the dropdown and play it directly — hero, deck, relics and
                consumables all come from the current project.</li>
            <li>Play the fight yourself card by card: check the Frenzy intent actually appears once the enemy drops
                below half health, and that Bloodpact's block shows up when you get hit.</li>
            <li>Adjust the card/encounter numbers on their tabs and playtest again — the loop is seconds, not a
                whole run.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>

    <h2>Effect steps (combat programs)</h2>
    <p class="muted">The building blocks of every combat effect program — what a card, enemy action, relic rule,
        status trigger or consumable can do.</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var (kind, label) in CombatProgramModel.AllKinds)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(label)</td><td><code>@kind</code></td><td>@StudioVocabulary.NodeDescription(kind)</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Targets (selectors)</h2>
    <p class="muted">Who an effect step can aim at.</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>Who it picks</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var key in CombatProgramModel.AllSelectorKeys)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(StudioVocabulary.SelectorLabel(key))</td><td><code>@key</code></td><td>@StudioVocabulary.SelectorDescription(key)</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Amounts</h2>
    <p class="muted">The ways a step's number can be computed.</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>What it reads</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var (kind, label, description) in StudioVocabulary.AmountKinds)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(label)</td><td><code>@kind</code></td><td>@description</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Conditions</h2>
    <p class="muted">What an “if…” or “repeat until…” can check.</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>When it is true</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var (kind, label, description) in StudioVocabulary.ConditionKinds)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(label)</td><td><code>@kind</code></td><td>@description</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">Values a “value compares” condition can read:</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>What it reads</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var (kind, label, description) in StudioVocabulary.CompareValueKinds)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(label)</td><td><code>@kind</code></td><td>@description</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Card selections</h2>
    <p class="muted">How a card-targeting step picks which card it acts on.</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>Which card</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var (kind, label, description) in StudioVocabulary.CardKinds)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(label)</td><td><code>@kind</code></td><td>@description</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Relic combat triggers</h2>
    <p class="muted">The combat moments a relic's injected rule can fire on (Relics tab, “Combat rules”).</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>When it fires</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var key in RelicCombatTriggers.Keys)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(StudioVocabulary.CombatTriggerLabel(key))</td><td><code>@key</code></td><td>@StudioVocabulary.CombatTriggerDescription(key)</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Run events</h2>
    <p class="muted">The run-level moments a relic reaction can hook (Relics tab, “When …”).</p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Technical key</th><th>When it fires</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var kind in RunEventCatalog.All)
            {
                <tr><td>@Cap(kind.Label)</td><td><code>@kind.Key</code></td><td>@kind.Description</td></tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

@code {
    private static string Cap(string s) =>
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(s) ? s : char.ToUpperInvariant(s[0]) + s[1..];
}
